  "updated": "2026-07-21T20:38:39.417009+00:00",
  "query_preview": ".. A stronger monsoonal push is forecast to occur across \nmuch of UT today as robust mid-level moisture returns to the region. \nCurrently, subsidence has built in across much of southern and \ncentral UT following this mornings thunderstorms. This will serve to \noffset convective initiation until around 1400-1500, enabling the \nenvironment to destabilize further with CAPE general ranging from \n1000-1500J/kg statewide, though CAPE ~2000J/kg appears possible \ncloser to southwest UT. With PWATs generally 1.2\" or greater in \nplace across much of UT (0.8-1.0\" PWAT anomalies for this time of \nyear), thunderstorms today are forecast to be efficient and heavy \nrainmakers. \n\nOf note, mid-level southwesterly flow will see an increase to around \n20-30kts with multiple embedded shortwave perturbations providing \nenough forcing for ascent. This will support multiple rounds of \nstorms this afternoon and through the evening hours as well as \nstorms propagating off terrain, which will be the opposite of what \nwe have been seeing through much of this recent monsoonal pattern \nwith storms remaining anchored to terrain. Typically, enhanced flow \nwould limit the flash flooding threat areawide as storms would not \nremain over problematic basins and burn scars long enough to produce \nflooding. However, given the efficient rainmaking status of these \nstorms with multiple rounds expected, the flash flooding threat \ncontinues to exist, particularly if redevelopment occurs across a \nsingle area in a short period of time. Additionally, the presence of \nbulk shear to around 35kts supports organized storms sustaining \nthemselves for longer. Additionally, shear on this magnitude is \nsupportive of a supercell or two, particularly across southeast UT \nwhere shear is greatest, which would increase the threat of flash \nflooding further. \n\nRegarding severe weather this afternoon and evening, the Storm \nPrediction Center has placed much of UT within a Marginal Risk (1/5) \nfor severe weather with an emphasis on severe wind gusts in excess \nof 58mph. Across most of UT, primarily western and northern, DCAPE \npotentially in excess of 1500J/kg will exist this afternoon as \nstorms begin to develop ahead of the initial shortwave. This will \nyield a scattered threat for strong to severe gusts through much of \nthe afternoon today as the enhanced flow aloft finds a better medium \namidst the higher DCAPE to mix down to the surface with. \n\nLater this evening across southeast UT where forecast confidence in \nwind shear to around 35kts is greatest, a westward propagating MCS \nis forecast to move into the area from southwestern CO. Some mid-\nlevel flow enhancement is possible around the time of initiation (to \naround 30-40kts) with 1600-1800J/kg of DCAPE in place. This supports \nan organized severe wind threat with a few severe gusts potentially \nreaching as high as 70mph this evening across areas such as Glen \nCanyon/Lake Powell and adjacent areas within eastern Wayne, \nGarfield, and Kane counties. \n\nA similar setup for flash flooding exists on Wednesday, though storm \nmotions are forecast to be somewhat slower as mid-level flow sees a \nslight decrease. Fortunately, this will reduce wind shear somewhat \nas well potentially reducing the longevity of storms. However, with \ndeep moisture remaining in place and weaker flow aloft, efficient \nrainmaking thunderstorms remain expected with a slightly higher \nchance of storms remaining anchored to terrain tomorrow. \n\nFrom Thursday on, mid-level moisture will see a slight decrease \nareawide. However, with ridging remaining overhead, daily chances \nfor thunderstorms will continue across much of the state and favor \nterrain as steering flow aloft gradually weakens. The forecast \nbecomes somewhat muddied as we enter the weekend with global suites \ncontinuing to disagree on moisture scouring, though the broader \npattern in place continues to support at least some continuation of \nthe monsoonal pattern into at least Saturday.",
